sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: coordinator 2026-11-02. Every effect runs in Swamp; a read takes the same path as a write. Decided 2026-11-02 (orchestrator): a read on a Database server runs as docker exec in its container (Q-DBAUTH); an external connection reads from 209 with the swamp role's clients; SQLite runs sqlite3 on its Node (R4). One step per placement; the Gateway picks it. No shipped script; the Gateway checks the statement and shapes the rows.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: database:query
  %% kind: database.read
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Database host<br/>WireGuard or ssh
  CLI->>GW: database:query shop<br/>"SELECT id, email FROM users"
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit database:query shop "SELECT id, email FROM users"`
  %%> - syntax: `<slug> <sql> [--write] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the slug of a registered Database connection, and one SQL statement of at most 16384 characters
  %%> Same kind:
  %%> - `database:tables`, `database:schema` and `database:describe` send a fixed catalog read through the same flow
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - write permission belongs to the `opt --write` block in the write chart; that flow also applies to SELECT with --write
  Note over GW: Validate, record the read<br/>connection shop · task #5170
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Database server app-mysql
  %%> - the connection's driver is mysql, pgsql or sqlite; redis has no SQL
  %%> - exactly one statement (`database.sql_multiple_statements`)
  %%> - its leading verb doesn't write, or the Gateway refuses with `database.write_required`
  %%> - for sqlite: the connection has a Node, or the Gateway refuses with `database.sqlite_node_required`
  %%> Where the credential lives:
  %%> - the Gateway stores the connection's password encrypted; at start it copies the password line and the statement into the Swamp vault as one value, `database-read-intent-5170` (R3)
  %%> - SQLite has no credential
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5170 recorded with the full intent; no lock, because a read changes nothing
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-read
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the client argv the Gateway built for the driver and mode, and the read host's address and SSH host keys
  %%> - the vault key name of the password and statement, never the value
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5170
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI waits with progress, as for other tasks; a read is a short run, usually a few seconds
  alt a Database server or SQLite
    SW->>N: Run the read<br/>@swamp/ssh · one client
    %%> How:
    %%> - Swamp reaches the host over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
    %%> - stdin is one vault value: the password as the first line, then the statement (Q-DBAUTH); SQLite has no password line
    %%> - the client runs no client command from the statement: psql gets it as `-c`, mysql runs with `--skip-commands --skip-system-command`
    %%> Where it runs:
    %%> - on a Database server: one `docker exec -i` into its container on Node 12; the client in the image logs in as the connection's own user, never root
    %%> - on SQLite: `sqlite3 -readonly -safe` on the file's Node, as the file's owner; it cannot write, attach or run shell commands
  else a registered external database
    SW->>N: Run the read on 209<br/>mysql or psql client
    %%> How:
    %%> - the `mysql` or `psql` client on 209, which the `swamp` role installs; 209 reaches the host directly, with no SSH hop
    %%> - the same vault value reaches the client on stdin
  end
  %%> Both:
  %%> - MySQL and PostgreSQL run the statement inside a READ ONLY transaction
  %%> - a 30 s limit; MySQL prints XML, PostgreSQL and SQLite print CSV; the output stops at 64 KiB
  alt the read succeeds
    N-->>SW: rows
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the client output; the Gateway keeps up to 500 rows, and more sets `truncated`
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + rows
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - the rows come back as the run's terminal result; its shared run reader sees it; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result<br/>task succeeded · rows
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5170 succeeds with the rows; no password or connection string in it
  else the server refuses or times out
    N--xSW: SQL error or timeout
    %%> When:
    %%> - a syntax error, a missing table, a refused write, or no answer within 30 s
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>database.query_failed
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - a bounded error code and message, without SQL output
    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>nothing changed · rerun is safe
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5170 fails; nothing changed on the server
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the command again; a read is safe to repeat
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5170
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + rows
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the rows once the task has ended; SQL null shows as `NULL`
  end
